  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Frequency Shifting",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert multiplication by a complex exponential in time into the corresponding shift of $X(j \\omega)$ along the continuous-frequency axis.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Shifting",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Frequency Shifting",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A complex exponential in time does not change the shape of a Fourier transform; it shifts the spectrum along the $\\omega$ axis. For a known pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, the sign on the exponential tells you which direction the shift goes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the known pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$ and multiply by $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$. In the transform integral, that factor combines with the kernel $e^{-j\\omega t}$, so the frequency variable appears as $\\omega-\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)e^{j\\omega_0 t}\\} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{j\\omega_0 t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the same transform as $X(j(\\omega-\\omega_0))$. So $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ shifts the spectrum to the right by $\\omega_0$ without changing its shape.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)e^{j\\omega_0 t}\\} = X(j(\\omega-\\omega_0))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The companion rule is the mirror image: $e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$ produces $X(j(\\omega+\\omega_0))$, which shifts the spectrum left by $\\omega_0$. Keep those two sign patterns fixed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\} = X(j(\\omega+\\omega_0))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The next examples will only use these two cases: apply the shift rule and read the sign of the modulating exponential.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Shifting",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Shifting $X(j \\omega)$ to $X(j(\\omega - omega0)$)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, find the Fourier transform of $x(t)e^{j\\omega_0 t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the known pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$. The cue is the positive modulating factor $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$, so use the right-shift rule from the introduction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)e^{j\\omega_0 t}\\} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{j\\omega_0 t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Combine the exponentials inside the integral: $e^{j\\omega_0 t}e^{-j\\omega t}=e^{-j(\\omega-\\omega_0)t}$. That is the same kernel as the original transform, but with $\\omega$ replaced by $\\omega-\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)e^{j\\omega_0 t}\\} = X(j(\\omega-\\omega_0))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So multiplying by $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ shifts the spectrum to the right by $\\omega_0$ without changing its shape. The result is the original spectrum evaluated at the shifted frequency argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Shifting",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Shifting $X(j \\omega)$ to $X(j(\\omega + omega0)$)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, find the Fourier transform of $x(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the known pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$. The cue is the negative modulating factor $e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$, so use the left-shift rule from the introduction. This is the mirror image of the previous example: only the sign on the exponential changes, and the spectral shift flips with it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\} = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Combine the exponentials inside the integral: $e^{-j\\omega_0 t}e^{-j\\omega t}=e^{-j(\\omega+\\omega_0)t}$. That is the same kernel as the original transform, but with $\\omega$ replaced by $\\omega+\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\} = X(j(\\omega+\\omega_0))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So multiplying by $e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$ shifts the spectrum to the left by $\\omega_0$ without changing its shape. The result is the original spectrum evaluated at the shifted frequency argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Shifting",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Applying a Numeric Frequency Shift to a Known Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $e^{-t}u(t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{1+j\\omega}$, find the Fourier transform of $e^{-t}u(t)e^{j3t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the known pair $e^{-t}u(t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{1+j\\omega}$. The cue is the positive modulating factor $e^{j3t}$, so use the right-shift rule from the introduction. This is the same shift rule as before, but now the shift amount is a concrete number.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-t}u(t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{1+j\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The right-shift rule replaces $\\omega$ with $\\omega-3$ inside the known spectrum. So the shifted transform is the original expression evaluated at the shifted frequency argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{e^{-t}u(t)e^{j3t}\\} = \\frac{1}{1+j(\\omega-3)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So multiplying by $e^{j3t}$ shifts the spectrum to the right by $3$ without changing its shape. The only change is the frequency argument in the denominator.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Shifting",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Choosing the Correct Shift Direction from the Modulating Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$, determine the Fourier transform of $x(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the known pair $x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)$. The cue is the negative modulating factor $e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$, so use the left-shift rule from the introduction. This is the sign-recognition version of the rule: the minus sign in time produces a plus sign inside the frequency argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) \\leftrightarrow X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The left-shift rule replaces $\\omega$ with $\\omega+\\omega_0$ inside the spectrum. So do not keep the spectrum unchanged and do not copy the sign directly from the exponential; reverse it in the frequency argument instead.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\{x(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\} = X(j(\\omega+\\omega_0))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the negative modulating exponential produces the opposite frequency shift. The mnemonic is simple: a minus sign in time gives a plus sign inside the frequency argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
